QuestionDB.register('behavioral', [{"id":"behav-1","type":"behavioral","num":1,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Ownership","title":"Tell me about a bug you owned end-to-end — from discovery to resolution.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Takes full ownership (not \"we fixed it\" without personal action)\n- Describes how they found root cause, not just the symptom fix\n- Mentions communication — did they notify stakeholders, update a post-mortem?\n- Shows systematic approach: reproduce → isolate → fix → verify → prevent\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Walk me through the Situation, what you personally did, and what changed as a result.\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Vague \"we\" language with no personal ownership\n- No mention of root cause analysis\n- Fixed the symptom but not the underlying issue\n- Never shipped a bug or claims bugs were always caught in review","tags":["ownership","debugging"],"related":["behav-2","behav-3"]},{"id":"behav-2","type":"behavioral","num":2,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Ownership","title":"Tell me about a time you missed a deadline or shipped something broken.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Owns the miss without blame-shifting to teammates, tools, or circumstances\n- Articulates what they would do differently with the benefit of hindsight\n- Demonstrates a concrete lesson learned that changed their behavior\n- Shows accountability to stakeholders (notified them proactively, provided a revised timeline)\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the original deadline, what happened, and what did you personally do to address it?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Blames others entirely (\"the PM changed requirements\", \"CI was flaky\")\n- Minimizes the impact (\"it wasn't a big deal\")\n- No lesson learned or behavior change after the incident\n- Claims they have never missed a deadline or shipped anything broken","tags":["ownership","delivery"],"related":["behav-1","behav-11"]},{"id":"behav-3","type":"behavioral","num":3,"difficulty":"E","star":false,"section":"Ownership","title":"When something went wrong in production, how did you handle it?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Stays calm and prioritizes user impact over assigning blame\n- Clear triage order: contain the blast radius → communicate status → diagnose → fix → post-mortem\n- Communicates proactively with stakeholders during the incident, not just after\n- Post-mortem mindset: writes up what happened and how to prevent recurrence\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Tell me about a specific production incident. What did you do first, and what did you do after it was resolved?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Panic-driven response with no prioritization\n- No communication to users or team during the incident\n- No follow-up to prevent recurrence (\"we fixed it and moved on\")\n- Waited for someone else to take the lead on a problem they owned","tags":["ownership","incident"],"related":["behav-1","behav-2"]},{"id":"behav-4","type":"behavioral","num":4,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Tell me about a code review where you strongly disagreed with feedback.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Stays curious — asks \"why\" before defending their approach\n- Separates ego from code; discusses the tradeoffs on merit\n- Finds common ground or an alternative that satisfies both concerns\n- Knows when to escalate calmly (e.g., ask a third engineer) vs. when to commit\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the feedback, why did you disagree, and how did the conversation go?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Dismisses the reviewer without genuinely engaging with their concern\n- Becomes defensive or takes the criticism personally\n- Caves immediately without understanding the reviewer's reasoning (\"I just changed it to make them happy\")\n- Has never disagreed with a reviewer (suggests low engagement or conflict-avoidance)","tags":["collaboration","code-review"],"related":["behav-5","behav-6"]},{"id":"behav-5","type":"behavioral","num":5,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Describe a time you had friction with a cross-team stakeholder or peer.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Proactive communication — addressed the friction directly rather than letting it fester\n- Genuinely understands the other side's constraints, incentives, or context\n- Reached resolution without requiring manager intervention\n- Takes partial ownership even if the other party was more at fault\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the source of the friction, what did you do to address it, and how did it end?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- \"They were just wrong\" — no empathy or attempt to understand the other perspective\n- The conflict was never resolved\n- Required manager escalation for every interpersonal disagreement\n- Blames entirely without acknowledging any role they played","tags":["collaboration","communication"],"related":["behav-4","behav-6"]},{"id":"behav-6","type":"behavioral","num":6,"difficulty":"E","star":false,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Tell me about a time you had to push back on a product requirement.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Frames pushback with data or user impact, not just personal preference\n- Proposes a concrete alternative rather than just saying \"no\"\n- Understands when to escalate vs. when to commit even if they disagree\n- Communicates concerns early (before estimation, not after implementation)\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the requirement, why did you push back, and what happened?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Always agrees with product regardless of feasibility or quality concerns\n- Pushes back aggressively or emotionally without a constructive alternative\n- No examples of ever pushing back (suggests passivity or conflict-avoidance)\n- Pushes back as a default without engaging with the underlying goal","tags":["collaboration","product"],"related":["behav-5","behav-12"]},{"id":"behav-7","type":"behavioral","num":7,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about ramping on an unfamiliar codebase or system.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Reads existing patterns and conventions before writing new code\n- Asks targeted questions (not \"how does everything work?\")\n- Builds a mental model systematically — understands the why, not just the how\n- Ships something small and validated early before going deeper\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Walk me through your first week or two. What did you do to get oriented?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Jumped straight to writing code without understanding the system\n- Did not read existing patterns and ended up reinventing or breaking conventions\n- Relied entirely on others and never developed independent understanding\n- Skipped reading documentation or tests as a source of understanding","tags":["learning","onboarding"],"related":["behav-8","behav-9"]},{"id":"behav-8","type":"behavioral","num":8,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about a technology or tool you had to learn fast under pressure.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Structured self-learning approach: reads official docs → studies examples → experiments in isolation\n- Timeboxes spikes and knows when to ask for help vs. keep digging\n- Delivered something functional within the constraint, even if not perfect\n- Retained and applied the learning beyond the immediate need\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the deadline, what did you not know, and how did you get up to speed?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Cannot recall learning anything new in the last year\n- \"I just Googled it\" without describing any depth of understanding\n- Relied entirely on a colleague to explain everything without personal effort\n- Learned just enough to unblock themselves and never went deeper","tags":["learning","adaptability"],"related":["behav-7","behav-9"]},{"id":"behav-9","type":"behavioral","num":9,"difficulty":"E","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about critical feedback you received and how you responded.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Remembers specific, concrete feedback (not vague generalities)\n- Describes a concrete behavior change they made as a result\n- Follow-through is visible — the change actually stuck over time\n- Shows they actively sought follow-up to confirm improvement\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What exactly did they tell you, how did you feel about it, and what did you change?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Cannot recall specific critical feedback (suggests low self-awareness or feedback-avoidant environment)\n- Defensiveness — explains why the feedback was wrong rather than what they did with it\n- Made a surface change but the underlying behavior didn't stick\n- Feedback was so mild it doesn't really qualify as critical","tags":["learning","growth"],"related":["behav-8","behav-15","behav-16"]},{"id":"behav-10","type":"behavioral","num":10,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Delivery","title":"When is code good enough to ship?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Balances quality with delivery — understands this is a context-dependent tradeoff\n- Names concrete factors: test coverage, blast radius, reversibility, user-facing risk\n- Has a framework for tech debt: ship now, document the debt, revisit with a ticket\n- Knows when to refactor before shipping vs. after\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Give me a specific example of a decision you made to ship something that wasn't perfect. What was your reasoning?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Perfectionism that blocks delivery: \"I never ship unless it's fully refactored and tested\"\n- No quality bar at all: \"Just ship it, we can fix it later\" with no risk assessment\n- Cannot articulate what factors they weigh\n- Has never shipped anything they weren't 100% happy with","tags":["delivery","quality"],"related":["behav-11","behav-18"]},{"id":"behav-11","type":"behavioral","num":11,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Delivery","title":"Tell me about a time you had to cut scope to meet a deadline.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Identifies what to cut vs. what is non-negotiable based on user value and risk\n- Communicates cuts proactively to stakeholders — no surprises\n- Documents deferred work (ticket, comment, TODO with context)\n- Revisits and completes the deferred work after the release\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the original scope, what got cut, how did you decide, and who did you tell?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Cut scope without communicating the decision to PM or stakeholders\n- Never revisited the deferred work — it just disappeared\n- Refused to cut anything and either missed the deadline or burned out\n- Let the deadline pass without raising the scope conflict early enough","tags":["delivery","prioritization"],"related":["behav-10","behav-12"]},{"id":"behav-12","type":"behavioral","num":12,"difficulty":"E","star":false,"section":"Delivery","title":"How do you handle competing priorities from multiple stakeholders?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Uses an explicit prioritization framework (user impact, deadline, dependencies)\n- Communicates constraints proactively — surfaces conflicts before they become crises\n- Escalates when genuinely blocked rather than silently context-switching\n- Negotiates timelines and scope openly rather than over-promising\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Give me a specific example where you had two urgent things at once. What did you do?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tries to do everything simultaneously and burns out or delivers nothing well\n- Picks a priority without communicating the decision to the other stakeholders\n- No escalation path — just figures it out alone until something slips\n- Over-promises to both parties and misses both deadlines","tags":["delivery","communication"],"related":["behav-11","behav-5"]},{"id":"behav-13","type":"behavioral","num":13,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Delivery","title":"Tell me about a time you drove improvement that nobody asked for.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Identified the problem themselves — not assigned or prompted by a manager\n- Made a case for why it mattered (time saved, reliability improved, toil reduced)\n- Shipped something concrete, not just filed a ticket and moved on\n- Got buy-in from others without having authority to mandate the change\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the problem you noticed, how did you decide it was worth fixing, and what did you actually ship?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Waits to be assigned everything — no examples of self-directed improvement work\n- \"I thought about it but didn't have time\" — ideas without execution\n- Made a change without coordinating with the team it affected\n- The improvement was trivial or only benefited themselves","tags":["initiative","growth"],"related":["behav-14","behav-15"]},{"id":"behav-14","type":"behavioral","num":14,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Delivery","title":"Tell me about working on a project where requirements were unclear or kept changing.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Drives clarity proactively — asks questions, writes down assumptions, gets explicit sign-off\n- Adapts the implementation without losing focus on the core goal\n- Keeps stakeholders aligned through the changes so there are no surprises at demo time\n- Designs for change when uncertainty is high (smaller commits, reversible decisions)\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What made the requirements unclear, what did you do about it, and how did the project end up?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Waits passively for clarity — blocked without pushing for resolution\n- Gets blocked on ambiguity and stops making progress\n- Ignores the ambiguity and builds the wrong thing (\"I assumed X and nobody told me otherwise\")\n- Becomes frustrated or disengaged when requirements shift","tags":["initiative","ambiguity"],"related":["behav-13","behav-12"]},{"id":"behav-15","type":"behavioral","num":15,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"What's a weakness or blind spot you're actively working on?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Genuinely self-aware — names a real weakness, not a strength in disguise (\"I care too much\")\n- Specific behavior they are actively changing, not vague intention\n- Concrete evidence of progress (e.g., \"my last three code reviews had no feedback on X\")\n- Understands why the weakness matters for their effectiveness\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What's something a manager or peer has pointed out more than once? What are you doing differently?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- No real weakness — interview-polished answer with zero substance\n- Vague answer that doesn't name a specific behavior (\"I'm still learning\")\n- Weakness that would be disqualifying for this role with no mitigation plan\n- Describes a weakness but shows no active effort to address it","tags":["growth","self-awareness"],"related":["behav-16","behav-9"]},{"id":"behav-16","type":"behavioral","num":16,"difficulty":"E","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Looking back on a project, what would you do differently?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Honest retrospective — names a real decision they regret, not a minor polish item\n- Articulates a concrete alternative approach they would take today\n- Shows growth: the retrospective reflects a lesson they actually internalized\n- Applies the learning to subsequent work (not just insight without transfer)\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Think of a project that felt finished at the time but in retrospect had a decision you'd change. What would you do now?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- \"Everything was great\" — no real retrospective or genuine reflection\n- Blames the team, the tools, or the timeline without owning any part of it\n- Cannot identify a specific decision — only vague \"we could have communicated better\"\n- The lesson is one they had to learn but still haven't applied","tags":["growth","retrospective"],"related":["behav-15","behav-9"]},{"id":"behav-17","type":"behavioral","num":17,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about a time you used data to make or change a decision.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Defines the right metric upfront — not just \"looked at analytics\"\n- Interprets data critically and doesn't cherry-pick results that confirm their prior view\n- Changes course when data contradicts their assumption (this is the key signal)\n- Understands the limits of the data (sample size, selection bias, lagging indicators)\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What decision were you trying to make, what data did you look at, and did it change your mind?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Relies purely on gut with no data to support technical or product decisions\n- Cannot name a specific metric they tracked or instrument they used\n- Ignores data that contradicts their assumption (\"the metric was wrong\")\n- Uses data only to confirm a decision already made, not to inform it","tags":["culture","data"],"related":["behav-18","behav-10"]},{"id":"behav-18","type":"behavioral","num":18,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about a time you balanced moving fast with quality.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Understands the tradeoff explicitly — names what they traded and why it was acceptable\n- Has a framework for assessing risk: blast radius, reversibility, user exposure\n- Ships with appropriate safety nets (feature flags, gradual rollout, monitoring)\n- Revisits quality after the fast ship — doesn't leave tech debt permanently\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the pressure to move fast, what did you do to manage the quality risk, and what happened?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Always moves fast and breaks things with no quality guardrails\n- Never ships because \"it's not ready yet\" — perfectionism blocks delivery\n- No framework for deciding which corners to cut vs. which to protect\n- Ships fast and never cleans up the resulting debt","tags":["culture","delivery"],"related":["behav-17","behav-10"]},{"id":"behav-19","type":"behavioral","num":19,"difficulty":"E","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"How do you think about customer impact when making technical decisions?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Centers user experience in technical choices — e.g., \"I cared about startup time because it affects first-session retention\"\n- Thinks through error states and edge cases from the user's perspective, not just the happy path\n- Has used user-facing metrics (crash rate, latency, retention) to guide technical priorities\n- Understands that technical debt and reliability directly affect users\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Give me an example of a technical decision where the right answer was driven by what users would experience.\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- \"That's the PM's job\" — abdication of any responsibility for user outcomes\n- Purely inward-facing technical thinking: performance matters because it's elegant, not because it affects users\n- No mention of users, customers, or the end experience in technical reasoning\n- Optimizes for developer experience exclusively with no connection to user impact","tags":["culture","product"],"related":["behav-17","behav-6"]},{"id":"behav-20","type":"behavioral","num":20,"difficulty":"H","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about a time you disagreed with a technical decision made by your manager or tech lead.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Raises disagreement constructively — uses data, benchmarks, or concrete risk analysis, not just opinion\n- Picks the right forum: private first, not ambushing in group settings\n- Commits fully once a decision is made, even if they disagreed — disagree and commit\n- Knows when to escalate vs. when to let it go\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the decision, what was your concern, how did you raise it, and what happened?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Never disagrees (lack of backbone or just telling me what I want to hear)\n- Went around their lead instead of having a direct conversation\n- Still harbors resentment — hasn't committed to the decision\n- Can't articulate a technical argument — just 'felt wrong'","tags":["influence","conflict","leadership"],"related":["behav-21","behav-7"]},{"id":"behav-21","type":"behavioral","num":21,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about a time you influenced a decision without having formal authority.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Built a case with evidence: benchmarks, user data, incident postmortems — not just conviction\n- Understood the stakeholders' incentives and framed the argument in terms they cared about\n- Knew when to push harder and when to plant the seed and let it grow\n- Got buy-in from key people before the formal discussion\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Who did you need to persuade, what was their concern, and how did you bring them along?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Relies on title or seniority to get their way\n- Gives up if the first attempt fails — no persistence\n- Can't explain how they read the room or adapted their approach\n- Influenced by steamrolling instead of genuine persuasion","tags":["influence","leadership","communication"],"related":["behav-20","behav-7"]},{"id":"behav-22","type":"behavioral","num":22,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Tell me about a time you worked across teams with conflicting priorities.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Sought to understand the other team's constraints before pushing their own agenda\n- Found shared ground or a solution that partially addressed both teams' needs\n- Escalated only after genuine attempts at resolution — not the first move\n- Maintained a good relationship after the conflict was resolved\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What did each team need, where did they conflict, and how did you resolve it?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Immediately escalates to management as first resort\n- Assumes the other team is unreasonable without understanding their context\n- \"Won\" the conflict but damaged the relationship\n- No mention of the other team's perspective — only their own","tags":["collaboration","conflict","cross-team"],"related":["behav-20","behav-5"]},{"id":"behav-23","type":"behavioral","num":23,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Ownership","title":"Tell me about a time you had to say no to a feature request.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Said no with a reason, not just a refusal — explained the cost (tech debt, reliability risk, timeline)\n- Offered an alternative or a later timeline, not a dead end\n- Got stakeholder alignment on the tradeoff, didn't just disappear the request\n- Stood firm when pushed back, if the reason was still valid\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the request, what was your reason, how did you communicate it, and what happened?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Always says yes — no ability to push back on unreasonable scope\n- Says no without a clear explanation or alternative\n- Lets the requester go around them instead of owning the decision\n- Changes position under social pressure without new information","tags":["ownership","communication","delivery"],"related":["behav-18","behav-6"]},{"id":"behav-24","type":"behavioral","num":24,"difficulty":"H","star":true,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about a time you mentored or grew a junior engineer.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Met the mentee where they were — didn't just give answers, asked guiding questions\n- Made time consistently despite their own workload — shows they value others' growth\n- Adjusted approach based on feedback from the mentee (teaching style evolves)\n- Mentee made measurable progress — shipped something, got unblocked, leveled up a skill\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the mentee struggling with, what did you do, and how did you know it worked?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only helped when asked (reactive, not proactive mentoring)\n- Did the work for them instead of teaching\n- Can't articulate what the mentee learned — mentoring was just pairing\n- No follow-up to see if their help actually landed","tags":["growth","mentoring","leadership"],"related":["behav-15","behav-9"]},{"id":"behav-25","type":"behavioral","num":25,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about a time you received critical feedback that was hard to hear.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Genuinely internalized it — didn't get defensive or dismiss it\n- Sought to understand the feedback before responding — asked clarifying questions\n- Made a concrete behavioral change as a result\n- Followed up with the person who gave feedback to close the loop\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the feedback, how did you react in the moment, and what did you do differently afterward?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- \"I've never received critical feedback\" — not credible\n- Defensive response — frames the feedback as the giver's problem\n- Acknowledged the feedback but never changed behavior\n- Can't remember a specific example — may not be open to feedback","tags":["growth","self-awareness","feedback"],"related":["behav-15","behav-16"]},{"id":"behav-26","type":"behavioral","num":26,"difficulty":"H","star":true,"section":"Ownership","title":"Tell me about a time you navigated ambiguous or unclear requirements.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Didn't wait to be unblocked — proactively sought out what they needed\n- Made explicit assumptions and documented them before building\n- Built an MVP or prototype to validate direction before investing heavily\n- Looped in the right people at the right time — not too early (waste), not too late (rework)\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was unclear, what did you do to resolve the ambiguity, and how did you decide when you had enough information to proceed?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Waits for perfect requirements before starting anything\n- Builds something large based on unvalidated assumptions\n- Never writes down their assumptions — just proceeds on vibes\n- Escalates every ambiguity to their manager instead of resolving at their level","tags":["ownership","ambiguity","communication"],"related":["behav-6","behav-23"]},{"id":"behav-27","type":"behavioral","num":27,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Tell me about a time you onboarded to an unfamiliar codebase quickly.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Had a systematic approach: read docs, traced critical paths, set up the dev environment, found a small bug to fix first\n- Identified the right people to ask and didn't over-rely on them\n- Made a contribution early — even a small one — to build context and credibility\n- Left the codebase better documented than they found it\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the codebase, how did you approach understanding it, and how long did it take to make your first meaningful contribution?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Spent weeks reading before touching anything\n- Asks the same questions repeatedly instead of building their own understanding\n- Relied entirely on a buddy and didn't develop independence\n- No strategy — just trial and error","tags":["growth","collaboration","onboarding"],"related":["behav-9","behav-24"]},{"id":"behav-28","type":"behavioral","num":28,"difficulty":"H","star":false,"section":"Ownership","title":"Tell me about a time you had to prioritize ruthlessly under deadline pressure.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Used an explicit framework: impact vs. effort, must-have vs. nice-to-have, reversible vs. irreversible cuts\n- Communicated tradeoffs clearly — stakeholders knew what was in and what was out\n- Made hard calls on scope, not just worked longer hours\n- Delivered something valuable rather than nothing — shipped a partial solution with a clear roadmap\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the deadline, what did you have to cut, how did you decide, and how did you communicate it?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- \"We just worked nights and weekends\" — stamina, not prioritization\n- Cut quality silently without stakeholder buy-in\n- No framework for what to cut — made ad hoc decisions\n- Missed the deadline anyway and didn't call it early","tags":["ownership","delivery","prioritization"],"related":["behav-18","behav-26"]},{"id":"behav-29","type":"behavioral","num":29,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"How do you stay current with Android and mobile development? Give a recent example.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Has specific, recent sources: Android Developer Blog, KotlinConf, Google I/O, specific Twitter/Bluesky accounts, newsletters\n- Can name a specific recent change and explain its impact (not just \"I follow Android blogs\")\n- Applies what they learn — integrates new APIs or patterns into their work\n- Shares knowledge with their team — tech talks, Slack posts, lunch-and-learns\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What's something you learned in the last 6 months and how did it change how you work?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Vague answer — \"I follow Android developers on Twitter\"\n- Can't name a specific recent change in Android or Kotlin\n- Learns but doesn't apply or share — knowledge stays siloed\n- Learns only through work tasks — no investment outside of assigned work","tags":["culture","growth","android"],"related":["behav-15","behav-17"]},{"id":"behav-30","type":"behavioral","num":30,"difficulty":"H","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about a time you defined the technical direction for a platform, codebase, or team. How did you get people to actually follow it?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Named a concrete decision they owned — not \"we decided to use X\" but what *they* drove\n- Built the case before deciding: prototyped, benchmarked, analyzed tradeoffs\n- Got buy-in from peers and senior engineers *before* mandating — persuasion before authority\n- Documented it durably (ADR, RFC, wiki) so the decision survives them leaving the team\n- Created a migration path and escape hatches — not all-or-nothing adoption\n\n**The key signal:** Can they distinguish \"things I personally preferred\" from \"decisions that genuinely served the team\"?\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the direction you set, what was the evidence base for it, and how did you bring skeptics along?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Mandated the decision by seniority without explanation — engineers don't understand why and quietly ignore it\n- Made the decision in isolation — others feel no ownership and resist passively\n- Never wrote anything down — direction lives only in their head and erodes over time\n- Confused personal preference with team benefit","tags":["staff","leadership","technical-direction","influence"],"related":["behav-21","behav-31","sd-20","sd-22"]},{"id":"behav-31","type":"behavioral","num":31,"difficulty":"H","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about driving a large-scale technical migration (architecture change, framework swap, platform upgrade). How did you plan it, maintain momentum, and handle resistance?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Phased plan with clear milestones — not a big-bang rewrite that requires stopping all feature work\n- Made the case in terms of user impact, engineering velocity, or reliability — not just \"it's technically better\"\n- Used strangler fig / parallel operation — new and old coexist, teams migrate on their own schedule\n- Found early adopters and demonstrated wins before mandating adoption\n- Created support infrastructure: migration guides, helper tooling, office hours, one-on-one unblocking\n\n**The key signal:** Did they ship the migration or just start it? What's the measurable before/after?\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the scale, what was your specific role, what was the biggest blocker, and what does 'done' look like?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Announced the migration and expected it to happen — no execution plan, no support structure\n- Froze feature development to focus on migration (unsustainable, creates org conflict)\n- Couldn't articulate why the migration was necessary — \"it just felt wrong\"\n- Made it all-or-nothing — no interop period, forced teams to migrate or be blocked","tags":["staff","leadership","migration","technical-direction"],"related":["behav-30","behav-35","sd-21"]},{"id":"behav-32","type":"behavioral","num":32,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about a build-vs-buy (or build-vs-adopt-open-source) decision you drove. How did you evaluate it?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Defined evaluation criteria upfront: cost, control, maintenance burden, fit — not just \"this library looks good\"\n- Considered total cost of ownership: build cost is obvious, long-term maintenance is what kills you\n- Timeboxed a spike/POC before deciding — theory is insufficient for complex build-vs-buy calls\n- Considered opportunity cost: what doesn't get built if we build this ourselves?\n- Made a defensible decision, not just the technically interesting one\n\n**Framework they should be able to name:**\n- **Build when**: core differentiator, unique requirements, OSS doesn't fit, maintenance burden would be low\n- **Buy/adopt OSS when**: commodity functionality, active community, team lacks deep expertise, speed matters\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What were the options, what criteria did you use, who did you involve, and how did it turn out 2 years later?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Always builds (NIH — Not Invented Here syndrome)\n- Always adopts OSS without evaluating long-term maintenance risk or fit\n- Made the decision alone — didn't involve infra, security, or legal where relevant\n- Chose based on hype (\"everyone is using X\") rather than fit for the specific problem","tags":["staff","leadership","build-vs-buy","decision-making"],"related":["behav-30","behav-36"]},{"id":"behav-33","type":"behavioral","num":33,"difficulty":"H","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about presenting a major technical investment — a rewrite, platform migration, or multi-quarter refactor — to engineering or business leadership. How did you make the case?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Translated technical language to business outcomes: \"20% faster CI saves $200K/year and 2 hours per engineer per week\"\n- Connected technical debt to user impact and business risk — not just developer annoyance\n- Anticipated and pre-addressed the top objections before the meeting\n- Offered options with tradeoffs — not \"we must do X\" but \"here are three paths with different risk and cost profiles\"\n- Got sponsorship from at least one senior leader before the formal presentation\n\n**A strong proposal contains:**\n- Problem statement in user/business terms (not technical terms)\n- Current cost (slowed velocity, incident frequency, engineer attrition)\n- Three paths: do nothing, minimal investment, full investment — with tradeoffs\n- Timeline, milestones, resource ask\n- Success metrics (measurable, not vague)\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What were you proposing, who was the audience, what was their biggest concern, and what was the outcome?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Presented purely technical rationale — leadership can't assess it and defaults to no\n- \"We need to do this because it's the right thing to do\" — no data, no business case\n- No alternatives — came across as closed-minded or having already decided\n- Didn't anticipate leadership's main concerns (cost, timeline, risk, team impact)","tags":["staff","leadership","communication","executive-alignment"],"related":["behav-30","behav-34"]},{"id":"behav-34","type":"behavioral","num":34,"difficulty":"H","star":false,"section":"Leadership","title":"How do you approach technical debt across a large codebase with many contributors? Tell me about a time you drove a debt-reduction effort that actually stuck.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Has a framework for classifying debt: intentional (shortcut to ship) vs. unintentional (ignorance) vs. bit-rot (entropy over time)\n- Prioritizes by blast radius and pain — doesn't try to pay off all debt at once\n- Makes debt visible and quantifiable: \"this component causes 40% of our crash reports\"\n- Builds a sustainable cadence — not a one-off \"debt sprint\" but 20% of capacity consistently\n- Gets it into the roadmap by connecting debt to business outcomes\n\n**What 'stuck' looks like:**\n- Debt has a dedicated allocation every sprint/quarter, not just when there's slack time\n- Tracking is public: tech debt register, tagged in code with context, visible in dashboards\n- Engineers feel empowered to refactor incrementally (the Boy Scout Rule is encouraged, not just tolerated)\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the debt, how did you quantify its impact, how did you get it prioritized, and is it still being maintained?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- \"We do quarterly debt sprints\" — isolated effort, not systemic\n- No data connecting debt to business impact — \"it just feels bad\"\n- Tries to pay off all debt at once — loses team buy-in and PM support\n- Debt stays in people's heads — not written down, not tracked, forgotten after the sprint","tags":["staff","leadership","technical-debt","engineering-culture"],"related":["behav-33","behav-35"]},{"id":"behav-35","type":"behavioral","num":35,"difficulty":"H","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about a time you raised the technical quality or standards for an entire team — not just one project. How did you make it stick?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Started with observation and data — not \"I think we should do X\"\n- Changed the system (processes, tooling, CI gates) not just the people — process beats willpower\n- Demonstrated, didn't dictate — wrote the first example, ran the first workshop, did the first review\n- Measured improvement: before/after crash rates, test coverage, review turnaround time\n- Had patience — cultural change is measured in months, not sprints\n\n**Concrete examples of raising the bar (any of these):**\n- Introduced a lint rule that eliminated a whole class of bugs\n- Added a required test coverage gate to CI\n- Created a code review checklist the whole team adopted\n- Set up a tech talk series that normalized knowledge sharing\n- Established an ADR process so architectural decisions are durable\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the before state, what did you change, how did you get buy-in, and how do you know it worked 6 months later?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Raised the bar by being more demanding in code review without explaining why — demoralized the team\n- Made process changes without buy-in — people worked around them\n- Can't measure the impact — \"I think it's better now\"\n- Did it for one project but it didn't spread — no systemic change, just local heroism","tags":["staff","leadership","engineering-culture","quality"],"related":["behav-34","behav-31"]},{"id":"behav-36","type":"behavioral","num":36,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"How do you evaluate whether a technical decision will hold up over time? Give an example where your prediction was right — or wrong.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Has concrete dimensions, not just intuition: scalability of the model, coupling to external APIs, team's ability to maintain it long-term\n- Applies the 'extension test': will adding the next feature in this area be easy or painful?\n- Has an example where they were *wrong* — this is more revealing than a success story\n- Knows when 'good enough now' is the right call even if it won't scale\n\n**Framework for evaluation:**\n- **Reversibility**: how hard is it to change if wrong? Prefer reversible decisions at the start.\n- **Coupling**: does this create tight coupling to something that might change (a library version, a third-party API, a platform behavior)?\n- **Extension points**: will adding the 3rd feature in this area be as easy as the 1st?\n- **Cognitive load**: will an engineer who joins in 2 years understand why this exists without finding the Slack thread?\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the decision, what was your reasoning, and how did it look 1-2 years later?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only has examples where they were right — no intellectual honesty\n- \"I just go with my gut\" — no framework, no transferable reasoning\n- Over-engineers for hypothetical scale that never materializes\n- Never revisits old decisions — treats them as permanent once made","tags":["staff","leadership","decision-making","technical-judgment"],"related":["behav-30","behav-37"]},{"id":"behav-37","type":"behavioral","num":37,"difficulty":"H","star":false,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about a time you had to stop or reverse a technical decision that was already underway — either your own or someone else's.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Caught it early — had signals before the full cost was sunk, didn't wait until launch\n- Gathered concrete evidence before raising the concern — not just intuition, not just their preference\n- Handled the conversation with empathy — the original decision-maker had to be brought along, not embarrassed\n- Proposed a clear path forward: what to do instead, how to migrate, how to recover gracefully\n- Extracted the lesson: what would have caught this earlier? What process would prevent it next time?\n\n**The key signal:** Did they have the courage to raise it even when it was socially uncomfortable? Did they bring a solution or just a complaint?\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the decision, what signal made you realize it was wrong, how did you raise it (especially if it was someone senior's decision), and how was it resolved?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Never had to stop a decision — suggests they either never make bold bets, or never raise concerns about others'\n- Reversed it unilaterally without involving the original decision-makers — creates resentment\n- Raised the concern but caved under social pressure without new information\n- No post-mortem — didn't extract lessons to prevent it from happening again\n- Describes a very minor course-correction — not a true reversal under pressure","tags":["staff","leadership","decision-making","courage"],"related":["behav-36","behav-30"]},{"id":"behav-38","type":"behavioral","num":38,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Tell me about a time you gave critical feedback to a peer. How did you approach it?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Went directly to the person — not their manager, not other teammates\n- Framed feedback around specific behavior and impact, not personality: \"the PR had three unhandled error paths\" not \"you're careless\"\n- Was private and timely — not during a group standup or code review in front of others\n- Checked their own intent first: is this to help the person, or to vent?\n- Followed up to see whether anything changed — didn't treat feedback as a one-shot event\n\n**What makes feedback land:**\n- Specific example, not a pattern accusation: \"In Tuesday's PR review...\"\n- Impact: \"This caused the test suite to take 40% longer\"\n- Request: \"In the future, could you...\"\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the issue, how did you raise it, and how did the person respond?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Went to their manager instead of talking directly first\n- Gave vague feedback: \"your code quality has been low\" with no specific examples\n- Never gives critical feedback because they don't want to damage the relationship\n- Gave feedback publicly in a way that embarrassed the person","tags":["collaboration","feedback","communication"],"related":["behav-4","behav-45"]},{"id":"behav-39","type":"behavioral","num":39,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Ownership","title":"Tell me about a time you had to deliver bad news upward — a slipping deadline, a quality issue, or an unexpected blocker.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Communicated early — as soon as the problem was clear, not on the original delivery date\n- Came with a plan, not just the bad news: \"we'll miss by 2 weeks — here's what we can cut to still hit the date, or here's the revised timeline at full scope\"\n- Quantified the impact: \"this affects the Q3 launch and the X integration timeline\"\n- Took ownership of the recovery, not just the announcement\n- Didn't minimize or bury the issue to avoid the conversation\n\n**What separates strong from weak:**\n- Weak: announces the miss the week it's due with no plan\n- Strong: flags the risk 3-4 weeks out with options — cut scope, slide the date, or add resource\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the bad news, when did you know, how did you frame the conversation, and what was the reaction?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Waited and hoped the problem would resolve itself — delivered news at the last moment\n- Minimized the severity to avoid a difficult conversation\n- Came without a plan — just dropped the news and waited for leadership to solve it\n- Let someone else deliver bad news they were responsible for","tags":["ownership","communication","accountability"],"related":["behav-2","behav-47"]},{"id":"behav-40","type":"behavioral","num":40,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Delivery","title":"Tell me about a time your estimate was significantly wrong. What caused it and what did you change?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Has a real example — meaningfully off (2x or more), not a minor rounding error\n- Identifies the root cause honestly: unknown unknowns, underestimated complexity, hidden dependencies, scope creep they didn't catch\n- Re-estimated as soon as reality diverged — didn't stay silent until the deadline passed\n- Describes a concrete change to their estimation process afterward\n- Doesn't frame it entirely as external factors\n\n**Common root causes worth probing:**\n- \"I estimated the happy path and didn't account for edge cases or integration work\"\n- \"I hadn't understood the dependency on another team\"\n- \"The spike revealed the chosen approach was wrong\"\n- \"Scope expanded and I absorbed it without re-estimating\"\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What did you estimate, what was the actual, what caused the gap, and what did you change afterward?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Blames others for scope changes without acknowledging their own estimation error\n- \"Estimates are always wrong\" — refuses to reflect or improve\n- Never re-estimates when reality diverges — keeps working toward the original date in silence\n- Pads all estimates by 2x without being transparent about it","tags":["estimation","delivery","planning"],"related":["behav-2","behav-11","behav-39"]},{"id":"behav-41","type":"behavioral","num":41,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Tell me about working with a PM who consistently deprioritized technical work. How did you handle it?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Translated technical concerns into business impact: \"this component causes 30% of our crashes, affecting X% of DAU\"\n- Built the relationship before trying to change prioritization\n- Found a creative compromise: embedded refactors in feature work, negotiated a regular debt allocation\n- Framed technical work as an enabler: \"if we fix this, the next feature in this area ships twice as fast\"\n- Escalated constructively if needed — involved their engineering lead, didn't go around the PM\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What technical work needed prioritization, how did you make the case, and what was the outcome?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only vented frustration to other engineers — never addressed it directly\n- Went around the PM to force prioritization — damaged the relationship\n- Framed technical needs in engineering terms the PM couldn't evaluate\n- Gave up and shipped increasingly fragile code without escalating","tags":["collaboration","product","communication","advocacy"],"related":["behav-6","behav-43"]},{"id":"behav-42","type":"behavioral","num":42,"difficulty":"E","star":false,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Tell me about working closely with a designer to implement a feature. How did you handle gaps between the spec and what was feasible?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Engaged design early — raised feasibility concerns before the spec was finalized, not after\n- Understood what design was trying to achieve (the user outcome), not just what they drew\n- Raised technical constraints clearly and constructively: \"this animation would drop frames on low-end devices — here are two alternatives that achieve the same feel\"\n- Proposed alternatives when something wasn't implementable, rather than just saying no\n- Delivered something faithful to the intent even when some edge cases differed from the comp\n\n**STAR hint:** \"Walk me through a feature where you worked closely with design. How did you handle gaps between the spec and what was feasible?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Implemented designs without questioning edge cases — left gaps for users to discover\n- \"Designers don't understand engineering\" — dismissive rather than collaborative\n- Raised feasibility concerns only after the spec was signed off\n- Shipped something visually off from the spec without looping back to design","tags":["collaboration","design","communication"],"related":["behav-5","behav-41"]},{"id":"behav-43","type":"behavioral","num":43,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about a time you advocated for your team's needs — time for tech work, tooling, or headcount. How did you make the case?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Identified a real gap with a measurable cost: slow CI costs 2 hours per engineer per week, on-call burden is causing attrition risk\n- Translated the need into business terms leadership could assess\n- Built the case with data and found allies before escalating\n- Was specific about what they were asking for and what success looks like: \"with this investment, we cut CI time by 50% and ship the backlog in Q4\"\n- Escalated to the right person at the right time\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What did you advocate for, how did you make the case, and what happened?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only vents frustration to teammates — never takes it up the chain\n- Makes vague asks: \"the team is overloaded\" without specifics or a proposal\n- Advocates once, hears no, and permanently drops it\n- Advocates for team comfort rather than team effectiveness","tags":["advocacy","leadership","communication","influence"],"related":["behav-21","behav-41"]},{"id":"behav-44","type":"behavioral","num":44,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about a stretch assignment that was meaningfully outside your comfort zone.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Describes something genuinely outside their domain — not a harder version of familiar work\n- Built a structured plan for their gaps: identified what they didn't know, found people and resources\n- Communicated proactively when stuck — didn't disappear into the unknown for weeks\n- Delivered — even imperfectly — and can articulate what they'd do differently\n- Describes real discomfort and how they managed it, not a polished success narrative\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What made it a stretch, what did you do to get up to speed, and what was the outcome?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only ever works on things they're already comfortable with — no growth orientation\n- Describes a project that wasn't actually outside their domain\n- Got overwhelmed and quietly failed without escalating\n- \"I just figured it out\" — no reflection on what made it hard or what they learned","tags":["growth","adaptability","learning"],"related":["behav-7","behav-8","behav-15"]},{"id":"behav-45","type":"behavioral","num":45,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Collaboration","title":"Tell me about a time a teammate wasn't meeting expectations. How did you handle it without formal authority?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Talked to the person directly and privately first — not their manager, not the broader team\n- Was specific about behavior and impact, not vague impressions\n- Gave the benefit of the doubt: asked what was going on before assuming intent\n- Gave it time to improve before escalating\n- Protected the team's health without throwing the person under the bus publicly\n\n**What makes it hard:** No formal authority. Others may be quietly covering for them. The root cause may be unclear expectations, personal circumstances, or role misfit — not laziness.\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What were you observing, how did you raise it with them, and what changed?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Went to the person's manager without talking to them directly first\n- Covered for them indefinitely — created unfairness the team would eventually resent\n- Made it a group complaint rather than a direct conversation\n- Couldn't give a specific example — just a vague sense they \"weren't contributing\"","tags":["collaboration","conflict","feedback","leadership"],"related":["behav-38","behav-4"]},{"id":"behav-46","type":"behavioral","num":46,"difficulty":"H","star":false,"section":"Ownership","title":"Tell me about a time you had to shut down a project you had started or championed.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Recognized the signal early enough to cut losses — didn't keep going purely to avoid admitting failure\n- Distinguished between pivoting (change direction) and stopping (shut it down) — made a deliberate choice\n- Made the case to leadership with data, not emotion\n- Documented learnings so the investment wasn't entirely lost\n- Moved on cleanly — didn't keep relitigating the decision or quietly undermining what came next\n\n**The key signal:** Can they separate their identity from the project? Do they describe stopping it as a rational decision or a personal failure?\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the project, what signals told you it wasn't working, how did you make the call, and what did you do with the learnings?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Never had to shut down something they started — always right (unlikely) or never killing things that should die\n- Continued the project to avoid the embarrassment of being wrong\n- Attributed the failure entirely to external factors\n- No retrospective — the learnings were lost with the project","tags":["ownership","decision-making","accountability","sunk-cost"],"related":["behav-2","behav-37"]},{"id":"behav-47","type":"behavioral","num":47,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Delivery","title":"Tell me about a time requirements changed significantly mid-project. How did you manage the impact?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Immediately surfaced the impact of the change: \"this adds 2 weeks and removes feature X unless we cut Y\"\n- Negotiated what to cut or defer rather than silently absorbing the scope change\n- Documented the change and its implications — didn't leave it as an informal conversation that caused conflict later\n- Didn't just say yes to keep everyone happy, then deliver late without warning\n- Knew when a change was minor enough to absorb and when it required a formal reset\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What changed, when did you find out, how did you respond, and how did it affect the outcome?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Said yes to every change without discussing timeline or scope impact\n- Delivered late without warning because they silently absorbed scope without re-estimating\n- Became rigid — treated any change as a reason to restart all planning\n- Required a formal process for even minor changes — slowed everyone down unnecessarily","tags":["delivery","planning","communication","scope"],"related":["behav-11","behav-40"]},{"id":"behav-48","type":"behavioral","num":48,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about a time you were burning out or hitting an unsustainable pace. What did you do?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Recognized the warning signs before full burnout: fatigue, loss of motivation, errors, cynicism\n- Took action: talked to their manager, reprioritized, took time off, redistributed load\n- Diagnosed the root cause: always-on on-call, too many context switches, unclear expectations, undervalued work\n- Made a systemic change — not just \"I took a vacation\" but \"we restructured on-call\" or \"I learned to protect focus time\"\n- Was honest with themselves and their manager about what they needed\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What were the warning signs, what did you do, and what changed as a result?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Claims they've never experienced workload pressure — low self-awareness\n- \"Just pushed through it\" with no systemic change — will hit the same wall again\n- Suffered in silence until they quit or made serious errors\n- Attributes it entirely to external factors with no reflection on what they could have controlled","tags":["growth","self-awareness","sustainability"],"related":["behav-15","behav-44"]},{"id":"behav-49","type":"behavioral","num":49,"difficulty":"M","star":true,"section":"Leadership","title":"Tell me about a time you disagreed with a technical or product decision but had to execute it anyway.","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Raised the concern clearly and professionally before the decision was finalized — not just after\n- Once the decision was made, committed fully and in good faith — \"disagree and commit\"\n- Didn't undermine the decision passively: slow-walking execution, seeding doubt in teammates, doing the minimum\n- Can distinguish a principled concern worth escalating from a preference they'd just do differently\n- Brought the same effort to executing a decision they disagreed with as one they championed\n\n**The key distinction:** Strong candidates are selective — they reserve escalation for genuine concerns, not every decision they'd have made differently.\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What was the decision, what was your concern, how did you raise it, and what did you do after the decision was made?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Claims they always agree with leadership — passive or low self-awareness\n- Raised the concern only after the decision was made — too late to influence anything\n- Committed publicly but undermined privately: told teammates \"I don't think this is right\"\n- Couldn't execute at full effort because of the disagreement","tags":["influence","leadership","disagree-and-commit","communication"],"related":["behav-21","behav-22"]},{"id":"behav-50","type":"behavioral","num":50,"difficulty":"M","star":false,"section":"Growth & Culture","title":"Tell me about joining a new team or company. How did you build trust and establish credibility?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Listened and observed before proposing changes — understood why things were done before suggesting improvements\n- Sought early, visible wins: identified and shipped something real before trying to shift how the team works\n- Made commitments and kept them — reliability builds trust faster than brilliance\n- Invested in one-on-ones — relationships are built individually, not just in group settings\n- Was transparent about their gaps and asked for help rather than pretending to know everything\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What did you focus on in the first 30-60-90 days, and how did you know trust was established?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Came in immediately proposing large changes before understanding the context\n- \"Just did my work\" — no intentional relationship-building\n- Treated trust as automatic — didn't see it as something to earn\n- Focused purely on technical reputation and ignored the human relationships","tags":["growth","onboarding","trust","adaptability"],"related":["behav-7","behav-44"]},{"id":"behav-51","type":"behavioral","num":51,"difficulty":"H","star":true,"section":"Ownership","title":"Tell me about a time you took over a project that was already in trouble. How did you assess it and what did you do?","answer":"**What to listen for:**\n- Assessed before acting — read the code, talked to the people involved, understood what \"in trouble\" actually meant before making changes\n- Separated technical problems from team/process problems — both usually coexist\n- Reset expectations honestly with leadership: \"this will take 3 more months, not 1\"\n- Set a new baseline rather than inheriting an unrealistic commitment and silently failing against it\n- Preserved the morale of the people already on the project — didn't make them feel blamed\n\n**The key signal:** Could they deliver hard news upward AND keep the existing team engaged? Both are required.\n\n**STAR hint:** \"What state was the project in when you took it over, how did you assess it, what did you change, and what was the outcome?\"\n\n**Red flags:**\n- First move was to rewrite everything from scratch — usually wrong and demoralizing\n- Inherited unrealistic commitments and didn't push back — then failed to hit them\n- Blamed the previous engineers publicly — destroys trust immediately\n- Accepted the project without questioning inherited timelines and scope","tags":["ownership","leadership","delivery","accountability"],"related":["behav-39","behav-46"]}]);
